<title>SWITZERLAND: Ciba ask Swiss to approve Bt-Corn.</title>
<headline>Ciba ask Swiss to approve Bt-Corn.</headline>
<dateline>BASLE, Switzerland 1996-11-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Swiss chemicals and pharmaceuticals group Ciba-Geigy AG said it has asked the Swiss health ministry to approve the import of Bt-corn, a genetically altered type of corn.  </p>
<p>Ciba said in a statement that Bt-corn protects against the corn borer, the world's most damaging insect to corn.</p>
<p>Bt corn has been approved in the U.S., Canada and Japan under strict regulations. In these countries it is approved as food meal for animals and can be planted.</p>
<p>Ciba said it was awaiting a decision by European Union (EU) officials on its application to import and plant Bt-corn in November.</p>
<p>-- Zurich Editorial +41 1 631 7340</p>
</text>
